Subject: AIT VirtSem: AIT & Physics

Nick Szabo poses some questions, only some of which can make a stab at:

> * How few bits does it take to describe the state of the
> universe at the Big Bang?  (Or as far back as we can go).

We won't know until some one produces a theory of everything, but some
people (like Hawking & Hartle) hope that with a TOE the solutions will
be forced.  If so the number of bits to describe the universe is equal
to the algorithmic complexity of TOE (which would probably be quite
large - even if it could be written on the back of an envelope, you'd
still need supporting explanatory, embedding context)
> 
> * How few bits does it take to describe the state of the
> universe at time T?  In other words, what is the function
> "f" in states(T) = f (states(T0))?

The Planck-Boltzmann equation says:  no.of states = e^(S/k)
where S is the entropy(T) and k, Boltzmann's constant.  But entropy
(like information) is a relative concept.  Relative to macroscopic stuff
like us it has pretty standard measures, given by the usual
thermodynamic equations.

> * Is [f]T>[f]T0 for all sufficiently large T?  (eg nanoseconds)

That's what the 2nd Law says (which I believe) because S is always
increasing with T.

> Did the universe perform some "computation" between time T0 and 
> time T?

Generally speaking I am unhappy to apply the word computation to
evolution of physical system.  Computations (which implies a computer)
are finite calculations.  Reality, by contrast, is infinitely subtle. 
An electron doesn't compute where it going.  It just _goes_.

> Going to other aspects of physics:
> 
> * When "information" is destroyed in an irreversible computation,
> which definition of "information" are we talking about?
> Traditional information-theory?  AIT?  Some other definition?

Everyone has to speak for themselves, IMO :-).  When _I_ talk about
information I mean information relevant to me.  So a process that I view
as irreversible (which may be reversible to someone else) is losing
information that I am aware of.  To some more perceptive onlooker no
information loss may occur, in which case they regard the process as
reversible.

> * Hypothesis: within reasonable time/memory bounds, evolutionary
> algorithms (GA,GP,artificial & natural selection, etc.) are
> highly irreversible.  (Intuitively, Darwinian selection proceeds
> by generating and destroying large amounts of information).

Sounds reasonable to me.  Darwinian evolution/'life' (but don't ask me
to define it - I don't think it has, or needs, a precise definition)
implies great entropy dissipation, irreversible acts, information loss
etc etc.  Same with GA/GP.

> * How does reversibility relate to logical depth?  Can 
> an irreversible computation produce structures that are
> "deeper" than reversibly-generated structures given a finite
> amount of time and memory?

Irreversible computations seem easier than reversible computations
(although this could be a function of our present gross natures), so
perhaps, yes.

> Nick Szabo                  szabo@techbook.com

