The AIT virtual seminar has been busy lately discussing the
(predictable) definitions of information, order, complexity,
randomness, and measures for all of them.

This confusion is endemic to these kinds of discussions, with several
different outlooks all competing. 

Here's a short summary by Tom Schneider, to show the same debate is
going on in other groups...and he makes some good points.


Newsgroups: bionet.info-theory,sci.physics,sci.math,comp.theory.dynamic-sys
From: toms@fcs260c2.ncifcrf.gov (Tom Schneider)
Subject: Meaning, Value and Function
Message-ID: <C9ELBM.9M5@ncifcrf.gov>
Summary: was Re: erasure of information and dissipation
Date: Tue, 29 Jun 1993 22:04:34 GMT


In article <1993Jun26.190625.5806@lut.ac.uk> C.E.A.Cook@lut.ac.uk (Charles Cook) writes:
| In article <C8u9Ev.A5J@ncifcrf.gov> toms@fcs260c2.ncifcrf.gov (Tom Schneider) writes:
| >In article <1993Jun18.110509.27617@lut.ac.uk>
| >C.E.A.Cook@lut.ac.uk (Charles Cook) writes:
| >
| >>It seems to me that we really need t be talking about this 'meaningful
| >>computation'.
| >
| >Shannon and other early writers were very careful to point out that the measure
| >of information they were proposing did not consider the value or meaning of the
| >messages being sent over a communications line.

| Thanks, I agree entirely! The distinction I was trying to make was not
| well expressed. Shannon wasn't inteested in the actual information, but
                                                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
| surely if the initial message is totally corrupted in the transmission 
| channel, then what comes to the receiver is 'meaningless' inasmuchas 
| it is not the message. There is no transmission of information. And in
| the same way, if a computation does not compute anything, or does not 
| produce an output, then it is not really computation. There is 
| absolutely no change in the information in the system.

The problem here is that you are using both the technical meaning of
information and the general English meaning at the same time.  This is rather
confusing!  By definition, Shannon was interested in the (technical sense)
"information".  He recognized that he could not capture the other senses, which
are approximated by "value" and "meaning".  It is intersting that the
corresponding senses in the biological systems lead us directly to Darwin's
doorstep.  The "meaning" of a binding site is that the organism containing it
survives.  If the binding site is not the right pattern, the genetic control
system may fail leading to death.  Darwin would have those which work survive.
So the idea of "meaning" or "value" becomes "function for survival" or
something like that, in biology.  I find it curious how this works.

