From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)

> An arbitrary string that is to be "compressed" (have a shorter description
> found) must have some computational model which can find the "structure." A
> very primitive compressor (a simple program) might only try "run-length
> compression," exploiting runs of 1s or 0s to achieve some slight
> compression.
> [...]
> A "smarter" program might look for things like the string being the binary
> expansion of some easy-to-describe number.
> 
> And a "genius" program might look for even more clever compressions. That
> is, much shorter descriptions.

I am still trying to improve my understanding of these subjects, too,
so I certainly don't claim to have any final answers.  But I believe that
AIT as Chaitin describes it does not include this notion of models dumb
and smart.

Rather, the algorithmic information content of a string S is _defined_
to be the length of the shortest self-delimiting program for calculating
that string.  This definition does not refer to the smartness of the
program.  It is perhaps true that we might choose to call shorter programs
"smarter" than longer ones which output the same string, but that is not
really part of the definition.

If we actually start to write programs which will calculate the AI content
of a string, then this distinction between smart and dumb programs is
relevant.  But that is just an engineering matter.

As an analogy, in principle every number can be expressed as a product of
primes.  But actually finding prime factors is not easy in general, and
the art of factoring numbers may involve smarter and dumber factoring
algorithms.  Nevertheless, this does not imply that the prime factorization
of a number is in any sense dependent on an environment or set of models,
even though some programs might give up sooner than others on searching for
the ultimate prime factors.  For a number with 1,000,000 digits we may not
be able to even know when we have completed the factorization.   

I'm arguing that the model-independence of AIT is similar to the model- 
independence of the prime factorization of a large composite number.  The
mathematics stands independent of the details of the implementation.

